      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A memory leak in an automotive control unit, that's a bit of a \"gobstopper\" problem to tackle. You're right, it's not just a matter of finding a single missing delete, but rather identifying the source of the leak. I'd recommend using a combination of tools to help you track down the culprit. \n\nFirst, you'll want to use a memory profiling tool like Valgrind or AddressSanitizer to detect memory leaks. These tools can help you identify which functions are allocating memory and which are not releasing it. It's like having a team of \"memory detectives\" working for you, searching for clues to solve the mystery of the missing memory.\n\nAnother approach is to use a memory leak detection library like MemoryLeakDetector or CatchMemoryLeak. These libraries can help you identify memory leaks by tracking the allocation and deallocation of memory in your code. It's like having a \"memory leak radar\" that alerts you whenever a memory leak is detected.\n\nOnce you've identified a potential memory leak, you can use a debugger like GDB or LLDB to step through the code and see where the memory is being allocated and released. It's like having a \"memory detective\" who can show you the exact",
      "source": "target",
