      "content": "You're right, manually wrapping every single call to `malloc` and `free` can be a lot of overhead, and it's also possible that a library is doing its own allocations, which would defeat the purpose of your wrappers.\n\nOne approach to detecting memory leaks without wrapping every single call is to use a \"canary\" value. A canary is a small piece of memory that is allocated and then immediately freed. If the same canary is accessed again without being freed, it indicates that memory has been leaked.\n\nHere's an example of how you could implement a canary:\n```c\n// Initialize the canary\nvoid* canary = malloc(sizeof(int));\n\n// Later, when you need to check if the canary has been freed\nif (get_canary() != NULL) {\n    // The canary has not been freed, memory has been leaked\n    // Log the leak and free the memory\n}\n```\nTo implement the `get_canary` function, you could use a static variable that stores the canary value:\n```c\nstatic void* canary = NULL;\n\nvoid* get_canary() {\n    return canary;\n}\n```\nHowever, this approach has a problem: it only detects",
